From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lean <lean@fnos.com>
Date: Mon, 1 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] PCI/pwrctrl: tc9563: probe synchronously on QCOM hosts

On Radxa Dragon Q8B, the QCOM PCIe host creates TC9563/QPS615 power
control platform devices and immediately calls pci_pwrctrl_power_on_devices()
from host_init(). The tc9563 pwrctrl driver currently prefers
asynchronous probing, so whether it binds before host_init() reaches the
power-on stage becomes timing-dependent.

With initcall_debug enabled, boot slows down enough that the pwrctrl
device often binds in time and PCIe/USB initialization succeeds. Without
that extra delay, host_init() can race ahead, see the pwrctrl device as
unbound, and return -EPROBE_DEFER instead.

Force synchronous probing for the tc9563 pwrctrl driver so the binding
order no longer depends on incidental boot-time delays.

Signed-off-by: Lean <lean@fnos.com>
---
 drivers/pci/pwrctrl/pci-pwrctrl-tc9563.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/pci/pwrctrl/pci-pwrctrl-tc9563.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/pwrctrl/pci-pwrctrl-tc9563.c
@@ -690,7 +690,7 @@ static struct platform_driver tc9563_pwr
 	.driver = {
 		.name = "pwrctrl-tc9563",
 		.of_match_table = tc9563_pwrctrl_of_match,
-		.probe_type = PROBE_PREFER_ASYNCHRONOUS,
+		.probe_type = PROBE_FORCE_SYNCHRONOUS,
 	},
 	.probe = tc9563_pwrctrl_probe,
 	.remove = tc9563_pwrctrl_remove,
